# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored — never commit real values.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application identity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Android application id and the iOS bundle id are per-developer (and per
# store listing). They are kept out of the repository so the public source
# does not carry anybody's handle. The committed placeholders in app.json
# (com.example.offlinefitness) are used when these are unset.
#
# Two apps can only be installed side by side with different ids, so give
# every build of this project its own value if you install several.
ANDROID_PACKAGE=
IOS_BUNDLE_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Maps
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The key is inlined into the app bundle at build time, so restrict it in the
# Cloud console:
#   APIs & Services -> Credentials -> your key
#   - API restrictions: Maps SDK for Android, Maps SDK for iOS (nothing else)
#   - Application restrictions: the Android package name above + its signing
#     certificate SHA-1, and the iOS bundle id above
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build environment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The npm build scripts (build:local, android:dev:*) export NODE_ENV for you.
# If you invoke Gradle directly, set it first to silence the
# "NODE_ENV environment variable is required" warning and to load the right
# .env.<mode> file:
#   export NODE_ENV=production   # macOS/Linux
#   set NODE_ENV=production      # Windows cmd
